  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 1208 AM CDT Tue Jun 23 2026\n\nOur area will be in a steady-state pattern with dominant upper level \nridging through the end of this week and start of next week. \nSubsidence with the proximate ridge will keep rain chances minimal.\nSurface pressure gradient across our area will be enough to maintain \nbreezy south winds each day. \n\nAn upper level high will be centered the northern Chihuahua state in \nMexico Wednesday and Thursday. Highs Wednesday and Thursday will be \nin the mid to upper 90s in our southern and east-central counties, \nin the upper 90s to 102 in the Concho Valley, and upper 90s to 105 \nin the Big Country. With the airmass drying out a bit, expect \nheat indices to generally stay under 105. Thus, at this time not \nanticipating the need for a Heat Advisory Wednesday/Thursday. \nNights will be warm and somewhat humid with overnight lows in the \nmid to upper 70s in the Big Country, and lower to mid 70s for the \narea farther south. \n\nThe ridge will become elongated from northern Mexico across Texas \nand the Gulf Coast to off the GA/FL coast on Friday. During the \nweekend, the upper high will become centered east of the \nMississippi River and build north over the Ohio Valley into \nportions of the Great Lakes. The ridge will still have an \nextension southwest into Texas. A large upper low and trough are \nprogged to move east across the western third of the CONUS during \nthe weekend, and then lift north into the northern Plains and \nCanada on Monday. Our area should have an uptick in temperatures \nFriday through the weekend with slight strengthening of the 850mb \nthermal ridge. Forecast highs are in the mid to upper 90s across \nour southern counties, upper 90s to 104 in the Concho Valley, and \n100-107 in the Big Country.",
